% Subsection HCAI

@book{shneiderman2022human,
  title={Human-centered AI},
  author={Shneiderman, Ben},
  year={2022},
  publisher={Oxford University Press}
}
La Inteligencia Artificial Centrada en el Humano (HCAI) es un enfoque que busca potenciar a los humanos mediante la integración de la inteligencia artificial (IA) con el diseño centrado en el ser humano. El objetivo principal de la HCAI no es reemplazar a las personas, sino amplificar y aumentar sus capacidades a través de sistemas que les permitan ejercer el control sobre la tecnología. Esto se logra mediante métodos de diseño de la experiencia del usuario, como la observación y la participación de las partes interesadas, enfocándose en el diseño de interfaces comprensibles y controlables.

@article{xu2019toward,
  title={Toward human-centered AI: a perspective from human-computer interaction},
  author={Xu, Wei},
  journal={interactions},
  volume={26},
  number={4},
  pages={42--46},
  year={2019},
  publisher={Acm New York, NY, USA}
}
Destaca la importancia de un enfoque centrado en el ser humano (HAI) en el desarrollo de la inteligencia artificial (IA), especialmente en la tercera ola de la IA. Subraya que los profesionales de la interacción persona-ordenador (HCI) deben tomar un papel principal en este proceso, no solo en el diseño de la experiencia del usuario (UX), sino también en la definición de sistemas de IA que sean éticos, comprensibles y útiles. La necesidad de un marco HAI ampliado que incluya un diseño éticamente alineado, una tecnología que refleje la inteligencia humana y un diseño de factores humanos es crucial para evitar sesgos y la desconfianza en la IA, haciendo que la IA sea explicable, comprensible y útil para las necesidades humanas. Los profesionales de HCI deben proactivamente participar en la investigación y desarrollo de la IA para asegurar que estos sistemas no solo sean técnicamente avanzados, sino también centrados en el bienestar y la eficiencia del usuario.

@article{chen2023human,
  title={Human-centered design to address biases in artificial intelligence},
  author={Chen, You and Clayton, Ellen Wright and Novak, Laurie Lovett and Anders, Shilo and Malin, Bradley},
  journal={Journal of medical Internet research},
  volume={25},
  pages={e43251},
  year={2023},
  publisher={JMIR Publications Toronto, Canada}
}
The potential of artificial intelligence (AI) to reduce health care disparities and inequities is recognized, but it can also exacerbate these issues if not implemented in an equitable manner. This perspective identifies potential biases in each stage of the AI life cycle, including data collection, annotation, machine learning model development, evaluation, deployment, operationalization, monitoring, and feedback integration. To mitigate these biases, we suggest involving a diverse group of stakeholders, using human-centered AI principles. Human-centered AI can help ensure that AI systems are designed and used in a way that benefits patients and society, which can reduce health disparities and inequities. By recognizing and addressing biases at each stage of the AI life cycle, AI can achieve its potential in health care.

@inproceedings{hartikainen2022human,
  title={Human-centered AI design in reality: a study of developer companies’ practices: a study of developer companies’ practices},
  author={Hartikainen, Maria and V{\"a}{\"a}n{\"a}nen, Kaisa and Lehti{\"o}, Anu and Ala-Luopa, Saara and Olsson, Thomas},
  booktitle={Nordic human-computer interaction conference},
  pages={1--11},
  year={2022}
}
Human-Centered AI (HCAI) advocates the development of AI ap-
plications that are trustworthy, usable, and based on human needs.
While the conceptual foundations of HCAI are extensively discussed
in recent literature, the industry practices and methods appear to lag
behind. To advance HCAI method development, current practices
of AI developer companies need to be understood. To understand
how HCAI principles manifest in the current practices of AI devel-
opment, we conducted an interview study of practitioners from 12
AI developer companies in Finland, focusing on the early stages of
AI application development. Our thematic analysis reveals current
development practices and identifies four main challenges: (i) de-
tachment of HCAI work from technical development, (ii) clients’
central role as the source ofuser requirements, (iii) uncertain nature
ofAI, and (iv) lack of value-based understanding of AI. The findings
inform the development of HCAI methods and implementation of
HCAI principles in AI application development.


